many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism questions along  these lines about revenge retaliation  resistance to aggression come up quite  frequently from people who are studying  stoicism and want to think about how to  apply it within the matrix of the real  lives that they are living and it's a  great question the Stoics do in fact  have quite a lot to tell us about these  but we want to make some important  distinctions for those who demand a  simple straightforward answer before  hearing the distinctions or the  discussion about them that gives us a  much better answer I'll just say this  the Stoics that leaves the classic  Stoics said that revenge is always a bad  idea it's it's going to entangle us and  all sorts of problems  and if by retaliation you mean the same  thing as revenge well the same answer  goes for that if by retaliation you mean  something like resisting aggression  against oneself or others for whom one  is responsible then there could be some  legitimate point to it so let's make  some distinctions right off the bat when  we talk about revenge we're talking  about imposing something that we  recognize as bad or evil upon another  person because they did that either to  ourselves or to somebody else that we  care about they might be you know fairly  similar things like when you say well  I'm gonna cheat on you because you  cheated on me they may be very  dissimilar but the idea is that we're  gonna punish somebody somehow and that's  going to make us feel better  retaliation might mean the same thing as  revenge for for quite a few people and  then we could also talk about resistance  and retaliation might fit in  that so when we're called upon to resist  what other people are imposing wrongly  on us or those that we care about or  those that were responsible for or those  who can't defend themselves then that's  something a bit different than revenge  even though we might be doing the same  physical action like you know punching  somebody or you know putting troops out  onto the battlefield or beginning a  lawsuit or firing off an email or  something along those lines it's the  motivation that really matters so we can  think about you know what is going on  with revenge what is a person attempting  to get out of it and then we'll evaluate  that from a stoic perspective and then  we can think about resistance and  retaliation might figure in on either  side so let's talk first about revenge  what is going on from a stoic  perspective when a person is desiring  revenge when they're attempting to gain  revenge when they've actually avenged  themself on another person so to begin  with there is a desire there and it's  going to be one that's going to be  within the modality of anger and the  Stoics distinguished a couple different  types it might also be you know hatred  of some sort if it bleeds over into that  but it's going to be within that  spectrum and the desire here is to  impose something bad upon somebody else  to make them hurt to make them suffer to  take something away from them to  humiliate them to have other people look  on and say AHA they they got what they  deserve so there's also a sense of  justice to it and this can be very  deceptive  the Stoics would say so we're you know  what are we doing in this case we're  damaging another person and we think  that that's going to make things better  we're also damaging ourselves in the  process the Stoics would say Epictetus  actually talks about this saying  somebody has hurt you so you think  you're going to hurt them in return and  that's going to actually make things  better they did the wrong  you're gonna make things right by doing  wrong so the Stoics really you know it  would say that that's counterproductive  not only in terms of making things right  but also in terms of your own personal  development there's there's a lot of  ways in which seeking revenge can really  screw you up  you know it also involves being too  focused on what the Stoics would call  externals and valuing them much too  highly not viewing them as what the  stomach is called indifference which can  be you know useful and in some respect  but as if they possessed good in  themselves and what might these be like  your reputation somebody you know says  something that that makes you feel as if  your reputation has been damaged so  you're gonna damage their reputation in  response the Stoics would say this is  really a loss of perspective in many  respects some people think that the  Stoics are going to simply say never  resist anything whatever somebody does  let them do and there are some passages  that you can find in various stomach  texts that seem to go along those lines  but they'll almost always be within a  context where some resistance some  rational resistance some thoughtful you  know some well-founded resistance is in  fact not just legitimate but sometimes  required of us particularly if we see  somebody engaging in injustice against  other people especially non Stoics who  aren't going to be able to take it as  well who are in fact thinking that  they're being hurt by somebody saying  bad things about them or taking things  away from them or depriving them of  their freedom all these sorts of things  if we see that happening Stoics would  actually say well we should call it out  and we should as far as we can resist it  you're gonna find Stoics not only  talking about this in for example Seneca  or in you know Cicero in his on duties  he actually  as that if you don't prevent injustice  from taking place when you could prevent  it harm happened in other people  you yourself commit injustice as a  result we see marcus aurelius concerned  with this and the idea is we're not  supposed to go looking for for things to  resist there's already you know some  cases where we probably do need to put  our foot down and you know if it happens  to be the case that we're placed in a  position where we do have to exercise  power Authority and tell people this is  wrong I'm going to have to punish you  then we might in fact even allow  ourselves to appear as if we're angry  although we shouldn't be angry this is  again Cicero and Seneca both suggesting  this this sort of thing  but notice what's going to be going on  there it's not motivated in the same way  that revenge is so some retaliation some  responses to aggression could in fact  fall under this this rubric of rational  resistance but we have to be very very  careful that we're not misleading  ourselves and slipping into something  that's more like revenge if you found  this answer helpful for you then please  share it with others who might benefit  from it this work is entirely supported  by crowdfunding on my patreon site so if  you find it valuable consider becoming a  supporter at patreon.com slash Sadler
 

